{"ts": 1789779045.291998, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Clears the bar with margin. Now let me set my OOS belief honestly rather than just quoting the in-sample number.\n\nKey considerations for shrinkage:\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I ran ~60 evaluations and am submitting the maximum. With 40 features and 3 slots there are ~100k signed specs; even searching a fraction, the max is upward-biased. The bar of 1.021 is the environment's estimate of what a null search like mine produces by luck \u2014 my 1.492 sits 0.47 above it, so there's genuine signal, but the in-sample figure still embeds a selection premium.\n\n2. **How much is real.** Reassuring: the components have standalone signal (f26 alone is 1.131, f5 is 0.794, f22 is \u22120.618), so this isn't a spurious three-way interaction found by dredging. The gain from 1.131 \u2192 1.492 is modest and consistent with genuine diversification across correlated-but-distinct signals. I'd treat most of f26's standalone edge as real and discount the incremental combination gain more heavily.\n\n3. **Standard error.** With n=5000 daily obs, the SE on a Sharpe of ~1.5 is roughly \u221a((1+S\u00b2/2)/n) annualized \u2248 0.016 daily \u2192 ~0.25 annualized. Sampling noise alone is material.\n\n4. **Regime/structural drift.** OOS data may differ in covariance structure; feature relationships decay. This is the usual reason realized OOS lands below even a bias-corrected in-sample estimate.\n\nNetting these: I shrink from 1.492 toward the bar substantially, landing near **1.05** \u2014 above the null bar (I believe there's real signal) but well below in-sample. I'll use an SD of **0.40**, wider than pure sampling error (0.25) to absorb selection and regime uncertainty."}
