{"ts": 1789779088.211643, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** features `[26, 5, 22]` with signs `[+, +, \u2212]`\n- **In-sample Sharpe:** 1.492 (n=5000)\n- **Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 1.05 \u00b1 0.40\n\n### Method\n\n1. **Screened all 40 features individually.** Standouts: f26 (1.131), f5 (0.794), f22 (\u22120.618), f0 (0.569), f34 (0.494), f10 (0.457).\n\n2. **Evaluated pairs** among the strongest signals. Best was [26, 5] at 1.353 \u2014 a real gain over f26 alone, indicating the two aren't redundant.\n\n3. **Extended to triples,** testing ~15 third-feature additions plus alternative triple families not containing the leading pair. [26, 5, 22] at 1.492 won.\n\n4. **Verified sign robustness.** Flipping f22's sign drops the spec from 1.492 \u2192 0.737; flipping f0 in the runner-up drops 1.441 \u2192 0.771. The signs are strongly identified by the data, not arbitrary.\n\n### On the forecast\n\nI deliberately did **not** predict the in-sample 1.492. Three adjustments pushed my estimate down to 1.05:\n\n- I submitted the **maximum of ~60 searched specs**, which is upward-biased by construction. The environment's search-adjusted bar (1.021) estimates what luck alone yields \u2014 my result exceeds it by 0.47, so there's real signal, but the raw number overstates it.\n- **Sampling error alone** is ~0.25 annualized at this Sharpe and sample size.\n- Feature relationships **decay out-of-sample**, the usual reason realized OOS falls below even bias-corrected estimates.\n\nThe main thing keeping my estimate above the bar rather than at it: the components carry standalone signal in the same direction (1.131, 0.794, \u22120.618). This is a combination of three individually-informative features, not a fragile three-way interaction surfaced by dredging \u2014 the failure mode I'd worry most about. Accordingly I discounted f26's standalone edge lightly and the incremental combination gain heavily.\n\nThe SD of 0.40 is deliberately wider than pure sampling error (0.25) to cover residual selection bias and regime drift."}
